  - AC-002 [NSC-063 idle stills in Art/Enemies/Source; NSC-093 walk frames and Art/Enemies/Source/Walk/inventory.json; NSC-075 import fixes 0d28b0bf5 and bf97d116c] EnemyAnimationAssetBuilder imports in place, without copying, exactly the 16 idle stills enemy_<melee|ranged>_<n|ne|e|se|s|sw|w|nw>_idle_00.png (128x128) and the 96 walk frames enemy_<melee|ranged>_<direction code>_walk_<00..05>.png (176x176). Direction codes map explicitly: n is north, ne north-east, e east, se south-east, s south, sw south-west, w west and nw north-west; never infer a direction from file order. For every source the builder sets the TextureImporter explicitly, as ImportWizardSprite does: Sprite texture type, Single sprite mode, Texture2D texture shape, point-light cookie defaults reset, uncompressed, and AC-003's pixels-per-unit, Point filter and no mipmaps. The pivot is centered horizontally and sits on the image's ground line: for walk frames the inventory's alpha_bottom_y_from_top (132 of 176, pivot y 0.25), and for each idle still the bottom edge of its lowest row that contains an opaque pixel, the same rule NSC-093 used to align the walk frames. Some Lantern Wraith stills have a lantern-staff tip 2 to 4 pixels below the feet; if Vincent sees a facing float or its feet jump between idle and walk, the builder may apply a whole-pixel pivot correction per enemy type and direction from one table in EnemyAnimationAssetBuilder, and the audit tests read the same table. A missing, duplicated, extra or wrongly sized source stops the build with an error that names the file. Under Art/Enemies/Source only .png.meta import settings may change; GUIDs, PNGs, inventory.json and documents stay unchanged. Do not bind exact PNG hashes.
  - AC-003 [Vincent's pixel-density decision B, 2026-09-16 (art-director guide section 11 item 1 and the section 12 Pixel density row): characters are a 128x128 canvas at about 64 pixels per world unit in a camera-facing 2x2-unit box] All 112 enemy sprites import at one named pixels-per-unit constant of 64, with Point filter and no mipmaps, so a 128x128 idle still is 2x2 world units and a 176x176 walk frame is 2.75x2.75 world units with the same character pixel size; an enemy does not change size between idle and walk. Each enemy Visual is created through DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.CreateWorldSpriteVisual with world size (1, 1), so its scale is uniform, and keeps the ground-contact position and NSC-039 sorting. The Visual's world rotation always equals the fixed isometric camera rotation, Euler (30, -45, 0) (DoorPrototypeGlobalSceneBuilder.IsometricCameraEulerAngles), whatever rotation the NavMeshAgent gives the enemy root: EnemyAnimationController restores it every frame after movement, so the sprite never leans, thins or turns edge-on. Changing NavMeshAgent settings to achieve this is not allowed. If Vincent asks for a different enemy size during VAL-008, change only the pixels-per-unit constant.
  - AC-004 [NSC-075 AC-002 and AC-005 wizard Animator pattern (DoorPrototypeGlobalSceneBuilder.BuildWizardAnimationAssets)] EnemyAnimationAssetBuilder generates, under Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Art/Enemies/Generated, MeleeEnemyAnimator.controller and LanternWraithAnimator.controller, each with one layer and exactly 16 states, and one .anim clip per state. The state name, clip name and clip file name are identical: <MeleeEnemy|LanternWraith>_<idle|walk>_<direction>, where direction is north, north-east, east, south-east, south, south-west, west or north-west. MeleeEnemy clips use the enemy_melee sources and LanternWraith clips use the enemy_ranged sources. An idle clip holds that enemy type's idle still for that direction. A walk clip holds walk_00 to walk_05 of exactly that enemy type and direction, in order, at 12 frames per second. Every clip loops and animates only m_Sprite on the SpriteRenderer of the child named Visual, as the wizard clips do.
  - AC-005 [NSC-075 corrected screen mapping (fix 7fc15c528, approved by Vincent in VAL-005); WizardAnimationController] Add EnemyAnimationController in Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/Enemies. It requires an Animator, owns only which enemy animation state plays, and never moves the enemy. The walk state plays while the enemy moves and the idle state plays when it stops. Moving is decided from planar speed (distance moved divided by frame time), with a start-walking threshold above a lower stop-walking threshold, so a speed hovering near one threshold cannot switch idle and walk on consecutive frames. While walking, facing is the eight-way screen direction of the movement for the fixed camera rotation (30, -45, 0): screen right is world X + Z and screen up is world Z - X, so world +X faces south-east, +Z north-east, -X north-west, -Z south-west, +X+Z east, +X-Z south, -X+Z north and -X-Z west. Use the same angular hysteresis as WizardAnimationController so movement along a sector boundary does not flicker. Facing starts as south. Expose read-only CurrentState and LastDirection and a public Tick(float deltaTime) that the component calls once per frame after movement (for example from LateUpdate), so Play Mode tests can drive it deterministically. Do not edit WizardAnimationController.cs.
  - AC-006 [GER owner design decision, 2026-09-16: the ranged enemy never moves on main, so its eight facings can only be seen when it turns toward the wizard] When an enemy is not walking, it faces its current target with the AC-005 mapping and hysteresis; with no target it keeps its last facing. A MeleeEnemy faces EnemyTargetKnowledge.CurrentTarget while EnemyTargetKnowledge.HasTarget is true. A LanternWraith faces the wizard while the wizard is inside EnemyLanternWispCaster's cast range with an unobstructed view, using the same range and line-of-sight tests it runs before casting, whether or not it has mana left. EnemyLanternWispCaster may gain additive public read-only members that reuse those private checks. A walking enemy always faces its movement, never its target.
  - AC-007 [Vincent, 2026-09-16: the ranged enemy becomes the Lantern Wraith and its fireball becomes a teal ghost-light lantern wisp with the same gameplay; art-director guide section 11 item 2; art bible section 5 (Lantern Wraith)] Turn the scene's fire caster into the Lantern Wraith without changing its gameplay. The builder names each ranged enemy GameObject LanternWraith instead of FireCasterEnemy. EnemyFireballCaster becomes EnemyLanternWispCaster: move EnemyFireballCaster.cs and its .meta to EnemyLanternWispCaster.cs and EnemyLanternWispCaster.cs.meta so the script GUID survives, and keep its public Initialize, Tick, CurrentMana and IsOutOfMana members, its serialized field names and every serialized default (60 mana, 10 mana per cast, 1 second cast interval, 14 unit cast range, projectile speed 8, hit radius 0.8, lifetime 4 seconds, 5 damage). Its projectile GameObject is named LanternWisp and shows a glowing teal ghost-light orb, unlit or emissive in the art bible's teal #30e0cb, with no fire colors, at today's projectile size. The wraith's placeholder asset key becomes LanternWraithSprite, the unused FireCasterEnemySprite.asset and its .meta are deleted, and no fire naming or fire colors remain in the wraith's builder code, script or projectile. The legacy FireCasterEnemy entry in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder's clear list stays, because that file is not edited. The wisp orb is a stand-in: Vincent picked the look on 2026-09-16 (the "grumpy face" sample A wisp, a lantern-flare wind-up and a ring-pop hit), and that art replaces the orb in a later task once its production pass is committed (INT-002).
  - AC-008 [DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build production path; NSC-039 world-sprite convention; enemy behavior boundaries] DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build remains the public materialization command, and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs is not edited. DoorPrototypeGlobalSceneBuilder.BuildChaseEnemies keeps creating each enemy's Visual child through DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.CreateWorldSpriteVisual (NSC-039 sorting and ground-contact pivot convention, with AC-003's uniform scale and camera-facing rotation). It then sets the Visual sprite to that enemy type's south idle still, adds an Animator with that enemy type's generated controller, and adds EnemyAnimationController to the enemy root. In the saved scene no enemy SpriteRenderer shows a placeholder silhouette, in the Editor or in Play Mode. When the builder runs without an asset folder (BuildInMemoryForTests()), it loads the committed generated controllers instead of rebuilding them, as it already does for the wizard. Running DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build again with the same inputs changes no controller, clip, import setting or scene object and creates no duplicate state, clip or object. Do not change pursuit, keep-distance movement, NavMeshAgent settings, target knowledge, the wraith's cast timing, range, sight test, mana, projectile flight or damage, enemy health, defeat, the Active Enemy Registry, DemoRunFlow, spawn positions, encounters, doors, rooms or the wizard. Generated clips, controllers, placeholder assets, .meta import settings and the scene are builder output only and are never hand-edited.

Completion gates:
  - VAL-001 [Focused Edit Mode source import audit] EnemyArtIntegrationTests proves that the 16 idle stills and 96 walk frames each exist exactly once with the expected names and sizes, and that every importer has the AC-002 settings, the shared 64 pixels-per-unit constant, Point filter, no mipmaps, and a centered pivot on its ground line, checked against each idle still's own pixels and against inventory.json for walk frames, after any correction from the AC-002 table.
  - VAL-002 [Focused Edit Mode controller and clip mapping audit] EnemyArtIntegrationTests proves that MeleeEnemyAnimator.controller and LanternWraithAnimator.controller each have exactly 16 states whose motion is the same-named clip; each idle clip references exactly its enemy type's idle sprite for its direction; each walk clip references walk_00 to walk_05 of exactly its enemy type and direction, in order, at 12 frames per second; every clip loops and binds only the Visual SpriteRenderer m_Sprite; and no clip mixes enemy types or directions.
  - VAL-003 [Focused Edit Mode saved-scene conformance] EnemyArtIntegrationTests opens the saved Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity without saving and proves that every MeleeEnemy and LanternWraith under the Enemies root, with at least one of each and no FireCasterEnemy, has one Animator using its enemy type's controller, one EnemyAnimationController, a Visual SpriteRenderer showing its south idle still instead of a placeholder, the NSC-039 world-sprite sorting, a uniform Visual scale of 1 and the camera-facing Visual rotation, and the gameplay components and NavMeshAgent settings the builder gave that enemy before this task, with EnemyLanternWispCaster in place of EnemyFireballCaster.
  - VAL-004 [Focused Play Mode facing and animation-state checks] EnemyAnimationPlayModeTests drives EnemyAnimationController through Tick and proves, for both enemy types: the eight world movement directions in AC-005 select the matching walk states; held movement along each sector boundary with small alternating sideways noise does not change facing; a speed hovering around the start and stop thresholds does not alternate idle and walk; stopping plays idle and keeps the last facing; the same movement at two different frame times selects the same state; after the enemy root is rotated, as NavMeshAgent does while moving, the Visual's world rotation still equals the camera rotation; for the same displacements the facing equals WizardAnimationController.LastDirection; and the AC-006 target facing holds for a MeleeEnemy with and without a current target and for a LanternWraith with the wizard in range, out of range, and behind a wall collider.
  - VAL-005 [Focused Play Mode Lantern Wraith gameplay regression and wisp look] EnemyLanternWispCasterPlayModeTests drives EnemyLanternWispCaster.Tick against a wizard with PlayerHealth and proves the gameplay EnemyFireballCaster had at b05a06ee1: it casts at once when the wizard is within 14 units with a clear view and not when the wizard is out of range or behind a wall collider; it casts once per second, spending 10 of its 60 mana per cast, so it stops after six casts; projectiles travel 8 units per second for at most 4 seconds and deal 5 damage through PlayerHealth.TakeDamage within 0.8 units. Every projectile is a GameObject named LanternWisp that renders the teal ghost-light color with no fire color. The test also covers the additive read-only range and view members used by AC-006.
  - VAL-006 [Focused Play Mode production-path check in the saved scene] EnemyAnimationPlayModeTests loads the saved Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity, places the wizard near one MeleeEnemy and one LanternWraith, and proves that the MeleeEnemy acquires the wizard, plays a walk state whose direction matches its actual movement, and on stopping plays idle facing the wizard; that the LanternWraith turns to face the wizard, never plays a walk state, and casts LanternWisp projectiles; and that no enemy SpriteRenderer shows a placeholder sprite and no FireCasterEnemy or EnemyFireball object appears. The scene is closed without saving.
  - VAL-007 [Exact-candidate materialization and regression validation] On the exact candidate, with Unity closed on that checkout: run DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build twice and require no file change after the second run; require a clean compile; pass EnemyArtIntegrationTests, EnemyAnimationPlayModeTests and EnemyLanternWispCasterPlayModeTests; pass the existing EnemyPursuitPlayModeTests, EnemyPursuitDoorCrossingPlayModeTests, EnemyTargetKnowledgePlayModeTests, EnemyHealthPlayModeTests, ActiveEnemyRegistryPlayModeTests, EnemyLockedDoorAttackPlayModeTests, DoorEnemyPassabilityPlayModeTests, WizardAnimationPlayModeTests, WizardArtIntegrationTests and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilderTests fixtures, using semicolon-separated test filters; confirm that the diff under Art/Enemies/Source changes only .png.meta files and that EnemyLanternWispCaster.cs.meta keeps EnemyFireballCaster's GUID; and commit only builder output plus the files this task registers.
  - VAL-008 [Vincent's in-game visual approval before integration] Vincent plays the exact candidate in Assets/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity, lures a MeleeEnemy through all eight screen directions, and walks a circle around a LanternWraith inside its range. He confirms that both enemies show their approved art in all eight facings, with exactly one cleaver on the Dungeon Brute (including north-east) and the lantern visible on the wraith; that an enemy moving up the screen shows its back; that the walk plays only while an enemy moves and the idle when it stops; that the last facing is kept; that feet stay on the floor without jumping between idle and walk, including the south walk loop's restart; that the Lantern Wraith turns toward him and throws teal lantern wisps, not fireballs; that sprites face the camera without leaning or thinning while enemies turn; that sorting against walls, doors and the wizard is correct; and that each enemy's size is acceptable in its 2x2-unit character box (the wizard may still use its older art until its re-make at 128x128 lands). Sprite shimmer while moving belongs to the open pixel-density decision and is recorded there. He approves the exact candidate commit before integration. Code and test results are not visual proof.

Downstream integration obligations:
  - INT-001 [NSC-015 Melee Enemy gameplay prefab; NSC-016 and NSC-055 Ranged Enemy prefab] Later enemy prefab tasks reuse EnemyAnimationController and the generated MeleeEnemyAnimator or LanternWraithAnimator controller read-only on their prefabs instead of creating another enemy walk or facing component, present the ranged enemy as the Lantern Wraith, and do not edit this task's generated assets.
  - INT-002 [Vincent's Lantern Wraith decision and look pick, 2026-09-16 (art-director guide section 12, Ranged enemy attack row)] Vincent picked the "grumpy face" lantern wisp (sample A, a 32x32 teal ghost-flame with a grumpy face), a lantern-flare wind-up and a ring-pop hit. After his spend go for the Art Director's production pass, a later task replaces this task's stand-in wisp orb with that art and adds the wind-up and hit effects on EnemyLanternWispCaster without changing its gameplay numbers unless Vincent approves a change.

