      "requirement": "All 112 enemy sprites import at one named pixels-per-unit constant of 64, with Point filter and no mipmaps, so a 128x128 idle still is 2x2 world units and a 176x176 walk frame is 2.75x2.75 world units with the same character pixel size; an enemy does not change size between idle and walk. Each enemy Visual is created through DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.CreateWorldSpriteVisual with world size (1, 1), so its scale is uniform, and keeps the ground-contact position and NSC-039 sorting. The Visual's world rotation always equals the fixed isometric camera rotation, Euler (30, -45, 0) (DoorPrototypeGlobalSceneBuilder.IsometricCameraEulerAngles), whatever rotation the NavMeshAgent gives the enemy root: EnemyAnimationController restores it every frame after movement, so the sprite never leans, thins or turns edge-on. Changing NavMeshAgent settings to achieve this is not allowed. If Vincent asks for a different enemy size during VAL-008, change only the pixels-per-unit constant."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-004",
      "reference": "NSC-075 AC-002 and AC-005 wizard Animator pattern (DoorPrototypeGlobalSceneBuilder.BuildWizardAnimationAssets)",
      "requirement": "EnemyAnimationAssetBuilder generates, under Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Art/Enemies/Generated, MeleeEnemyAnimator.controller and LanternWraithAnimator.controller, each with one layer and exactly 16 states, and one .anim clip per state. The state name, clip name and clip file name are identical: <MeleeEnemy|LanternWraith>_<idle|walk>_<direction>, where direction is north, north-east, east, south-east, south, south-west, west or north-west. MeleeEnemy clips use the enemy_melee sources and LanternWraith clips use the enemy_ranged sources. An idle clip holds that enemy type's idle still for that direction. A walk clip holds walk_00 to walk_05 of exactly that enemy type and direction, in order, at 12 frames per second. Every clip loops and animates only m_Sprite on the SpriteRenderer of the child named Visual, as the wizard clips do."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-005",
      "reference": "NSC-075 corrected screen mapping (fix 7fc15c528, approved by Vincent in VAL-005); WizardAnimationController",
      "requirement": "Add EnemyAnimationController in Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/Enemies. It requires an Animator, owns only which enemy animation state plays, and never moves the enemy. The walk state plays while the enemy moves and the idle state plays when it stops. Moving is decided from planar speed (distance moved divided by frame time), with a start-walking threshold above a lower stop-walking threshold, so a speed hovering near one threshold cannot switch idle and walk on consecutive frames. While walking, facing is the eight-way screen direction of the movement for the fixed camera rotation (30, -45, 0): screen right is world X + Z and screen up is world Z - X, so world +X faces south-east, +Z north-east, -X north-west, -Z south-west, +X+Z east, +X-Z south, -X+Z north and -X-Z west. Use the same angular hysteresis as WizardAnimationController so movement along a sector boundary does not flicker. Facing starts as south. Expose read-only CurrentState and LastDirection and a public Tick(float deltaTime) that the component calls once per frame after movement (for example from LateUpdate), so Play Mode tests can drive it deterministically. Do not edit WizardAnimationController.cs."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-006",
      "reference": "GER owner design decision, 2026-09-16: the ranged enemy never moves on main, so its eight facings can only be seen when it turns toward the wizard",
      "requirement": "When an enemy is not walking, it faces its current target with the AC-005 mapping and hysteresis; with no target it keeps its last facing. A MeleeEnemy faces EnemyTargetKnowledge.CurrentTarget while EnemyTargetKnowledge.HasTarget is true. A LanternWraith faces the wizard while the wizard is inside EnemyLanternWispCaster's cast range with an unobstructed view, using the same range and line-of-sight tests it runs before casting, whether or not it has mana left. EnemyLanternWispCaster may gain additive public read-only members that reuse those private checks. A walking enemy always faces its movement, never its target."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-007",
      "reference": "Vincent, 2026-09-16: the ranged enemy becomes the Lantern Wraith and its fireball becomes a teal ghost-light lantern wisp with the same gameplay; art-director guide section 11 item 2; art bible section 5 (Lantern Wraith)",
      "requirement": "Turn the scene's fire caster into the Lantern Wraith without changing its gameplay. The builder names each ranged enemy GameObject LanternWraith instead of FireCasterEnemy. EnemyFireballCaster becomes EnemyLanternWispCaster: move EnemyFireballCaster.cs and its .meta to EnemyLanternWispCaster.cs and EnemyLanternWispCaster.cs.meta so the script GUID survives, and keep its public Initialize, Tick, CurrentMana and IsOutOfMana members, its serialized field names and every serialized default (60 mana, 10 mana per cast, 1 second cast interval, 14 unit cast range, projectile speed 8, hit radius 0.8, lifetime 4 seconds, 5 damage). Its projectile GameObject is named LanternWisp and shows a glowing teal ghost-light orb, unlit or emissive in the art bible's teal #30e0cb, with no fire colors, at today's projectile size. The wraith's placeholder asset key becomes LanternWraithSprite, the unused FireCasterEnemySprite.asset and its .meta are deleted, and no fire naming or fire colors remain in the wraith's builder code, script or projectile. The legacy FireCasterEnemy entry in DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder's clear list stays, because that file is not edited. The wisp orb is a stand-in: Vincent picked the look on 2026-09-16 (the \"grumpy face\" sample A wisp, a lantern-flare wind-up and a ring-pop hit), and that art replaces the orb in a later task once its production pass is committed (INT-002)."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-008",
      "reference": "DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build production path; NSC-039 world-sprite convention; enemy behavior boundaries",
      "requirement": "DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build remains the public materialization command, and DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs is not edited. DoorPrototypeGlobalSceneBuilder.BuildChaseEnemies keeps creating each enemy's Visual child through DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.CreateWorldSpriteVisual (NSC-039 sorting and ground-contact pivot convention, with AC-003's uniform scale and camera-facing rotation). It then sets the Visual sprite to that enemy type's south idle still, adds an Animator with that enemy type's generated controller, and adds EnemyAnimationController to the enemy root. In the saved scene no enemy SpriteRenderer shows a placeholder silhouette, in the Editor or in Play Mode. When the builder runs without an asset folder (BuildInMemoryForTests()), it loads the committed generated controllers instead of rebuilding them, as it already does for the wizard. Running DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.Build again with the same inputs changes no controller, clip, import setting or scene object and creates no duplicate state, clip or object. Do not change pursuit, keep-distance movement, NavMeshAgent settings, target knowledge, the wraith's cast timing, range, sight test, mana, projectile flight or damage, enemy health, defeat, the Active Enemy Registry, DemoRunFlow, spawn positions, encounters, doors, rooms or the wizard. Generated clips, controllers, placeholder assets, .meta import settings and the scene are builder output only and are never hand-edited."
    }
